/**
@mainpage FyuuAsset API Guide

@tableofcontents

FyuuAsset imports and persists dynamically typed assets. Asset types are open
UTF-8 strings rather than a closed enumeration. Every asset has one stable
fyuu_asset::UUID, one runtime type name, one workspace-wide name, and an owned
fyuu_asset::Object containing its type-specific fields.

@section context Context and diagnostics

Call fyuu_asset::InitializeAssetContext before importing, loading, or deleting
assets. The first call establishes the process-wide normalized asset root. The
optional fyuu_asset::log::Sink is borrowed, may receive synchronous calls from
worker threads, and must outlive all asset-system use.

@code
fyuu_asset::InitializeAssetContext("assets", &sink);
@endcode

@section values Dynamic values

fyuu_asset::Value owns null, boolean, signed or unsigned 64-bit integer,
floating-point, string, fyuu_asset::Binary, fyuu_asset::File,
fyuu_asset::Array, fyuu_asset::Object, or fyuu_asset::UUID. Is<T>() checks the
active alternative and As<T>() accesses it. A type mismatch throws
std::bad_variant_access.

Binary data is encoded inside the asset JSON. File values are persisted beside
the JSON in a category directory selected from the logical filename. UUID
values express identity without retaining or loading the referenced asset.

@section import Import

fyuu_asset::Importer is an awaitable parser for a regular file, directory, or
ZIP package. Awaiting it returns a neutral fyuu_asset::Object.
fyuu_asset::Factory persists the complete field object selected by the
application. Both operations run on the shared asset worker.

@code
fyuu_asset::Importer::Register("miku_model", ParseMikuModel);
auto fields = co_await fyuu_asset::Importer{"miku_model", "sources/character.zip"};
auto asset = co_await fyuu_asset::Factory{"miku_model", "miku", fields};
auto uuid = asset.GetUUID();
@endcode

The registered ImportProtocol receives only a public read-only ImportFileSystem.
It discovers UTF-8 virtual entries with ListFiles(), reads entries and their
dependencies with Read(), and never sees host paths. RegisterBuiltIn selects
FyuuAsset's format-detecting parser.

A package can select one entry with `entry` or several entries with `entries`
in its `asset.json`. Without a manifest, the importer discovers supported model
entries or one unambiguous JSON entry. Virtual package paths are UTF-8.

JSON becomes dynamic fields. Supported models are parsed with Assimp, with an
internal PMD fallback. Model textures are decoded, mipmapped, and converted to
BC data for Runtime upload. Unknown formats are retained in the `binary` field.

@section importer_fields Built-in importer fields

The value system is open. Built-in parsers produce the following neutral output
protocols; these are not Asset schemas:

- A JSON object keeps its own top-level fields.
- An unknown format or standalone binary/image produces `binary`.
- One model produces `geometry`, `meshes`, `materials`, `textures`, and
  `animation_count`.
- Multiple model entries produce `models`; each model object also has a stable
  `name` equal to `model_N`.

`geometry` is one File containing `geometry.mesh`. Mesh stream fields contain
byte `offset` and `size` ranges into this file. Assimp meshes contain `name`,
`material`, `vertex_count`, `index_count`, `positions`, and `indices`, plus
conditional `normals`, `texcoords`, `tangents`, and `bitangents`. `material`
indexes the model's `materials` array. Vector streams are packed float3, UVs are
float2, and indices are std::uint32_t.

The PMD fallback also emits two std::uint32_t `bone_indices` and float2
`bone_weights` per vertex. Assimp materials contain `name` and `textures`.
Each binding contains numeric Assimp `semantic` and `texture`, an index into the
model's `textures` array. PMD materials additionally contain `diffuse`, `alpha`,
`specularity`, `specular`, and `ambient`.

Texture objects contain `name`, `width`, `height`, `format`, `mip_levels`, and
`pixels`. Each mip has `width`, `height`, byte `offset`, and byte `size` into the
pixels File. Supported stored formats are BC1, BC3, and BC5 variants named in
README.md.

`animation_count` is currently metadata only. Assimp animation channels,
nodes, and skeleton data are not retained. Standalone images currently produce
`binary`; only model-referenced textures follow the BC conversion path.

@section load Load and field access

Factory loads by stable UUID or by runtime type and name. Both forms are native
awaitables executed on the asset worker.

@code
auto asset = co_await fyuu_asset::Factory{"miku_model", "miku"};
auto& meshes = asset.GetField("meshes").As<fyuu_asset::Array>();
@endcode

GetField() preserves the Asset object's constness and value category. A missing
field throws std::out_of_range.

@section deletion Delete

Delete by UUID or by runtime type and name. Awaiting Deleter returns the UUID
that was removed. Catalog removal is committed before the independent asset
directory is cleaned. Existing in-memory Asset snapshots cannot recreate a
deleted catalog entry during destruction.

@code
auto deleted_uuid = co_await fyuu_asset::Deleter{uuid};
@endcode

If directory cleanup fails after the catalog commit, the awaitable reports the
filesystem exception while the identity remains deleted.

@section lifetime Lifetime and persistence

fyuu_asset::Asset is copyable and movable. Mutable GetField() access can edit
fields. Destruction submits the final snapshot asynchronously; moving transfers
that persistence responsibility. Copies are independent snapshots, not
concurrent edit transactions, so the last executed save becomes persistent.

A successful Factory creation persists both fields and catalog identity.
Importer only parses source data and never changes the catalog.
Destruction-time persistence is noexcept and cannot report a background failure
to the destroying caller.

@section storage Storage layout

Each asset name owns one workspace-wide directory. Type is catalog metadata and
does not introduce another directory level.

@code
asset_root/
  catalog.json
  miku/
    asset.json
    mesh/
    texture/
    anime/
    skeleton/
@endcode

catalog.json stores only `uuid`, `type`, and `name`. asset.json stores dynamic
fields with internal type tags. Those tags are persistence details rather than
a public interchange schema. Catalog changes use native durable replacement on
Windows and POSIX platforms.

@section ownership Runtime ownership

FyuuAsset owns CPU-side import, dynamic representation, and persistence. It does
not create RHI resources. Runtime loads UUID references according to demand and
owns loaded-asset and GPU-resource caches.
*/
